# windows-render -- render.dll's own entry, selected by ANSICON_DEF=render.
#
# Two files, one entry. `windows-render.caps` is the copy a running session actually reads (jline loads it
# from its own resources and from the shipped jar); this file is the same entry in the form `tic` and
# `infocmp` understand, so the set can be compiled and round-tripped. `terminfo_check.sh` proves the two
# agree, that the compile is clean, and that the shipped copies are byte-identical to the mirror. Change
# one and you change all three; the gate is the thing that says whether you did.
#
# What is deliberately not in here, and why, is spelled out in `TERMINFO.md`. The short version: a
# capability that describes the *other* parser a session might be read by is a claim about something this
# renderer does not control, so `smam`/`rmam`, `smir`/`rmir`, `hts`/`tbc`, `initc`/`ccc` and `flash` stay
# out even though the renderer models some of them. `xenl` is absent because it was measured absent.
# `kmous` is in: the reports it names are produced here, and a session that selects another report
# encoding says so with its own mode.
#
# Values are written the way jline's own reader expects, so a comparison is made against bytes, not
# against a plausible-looking string: `caps_check.ps1` expands every one of them through the reader's
# decoder and asserts what reaches the wire.

windows-render|render.dll's own entry, forked from windows-conemu (same dialect at the fork, free to diverge): ANSI as the native renderer paints it (margin wrap is immediate, so no xenl; every erase fills with the colours on the pen; the replies it advertises are replies it really sends),
